window.ME_DATA = window.ME_DATA || {};
window.ME_DATA["y13-me"] = [
 {
  id:"l1", lesson:1, title:"What Is Corruption and Where Does It Occur?", unit:"Unit 1: Ethics in Our Contemporary Life", art:"power",
  dna:"A company director offers a government official a luxury watch “as a gesture of friendship” one week before the official decides which company wins a large contract. Write down: is this corruption? What exactly makes it right or wrong — the gift itself, the timing, or the power the official holds?",
  dnaQs:["Is the watch a gift or a bribe? Give one reason.","What matters most here: the gift itself, the timing, or the official's power?","Write your own one-sentence definition of corruption."],
  dnaHint:"A strong answer notices that the timing and the official's decision-making power turn a ‘gift’ into a likely bribe: the director expects something in return. A good definition mentions three parts: power that has been entrusted to someone, an abuse of that power, and a private benefit for the person or for people close to them.",
  intentions:["Understand corruption as the abuse of entrusted power for private gain.","Distinguish between the main forms of corruption, such as bribery, embezzlement, nepotism and conflicts of interest.","Analyse where corruption occurs across the public and private sectors, and why some sectors carry higher risk."],
  criteria:["I can define corruption precisely and explain each part of the definition.","I can identify and distinguish between at least four forms of corruption in real or realistic cases.","I can analyse why certain sectors and decision points carry a higher risk of corruption."],
  concept:{key:"Systems", related:"Power, Accountability", soi:"Corruption occurs wherever entrusted power can be abused for private gain, so understanding its forms and locations is the first step towards preventing it.", context:"Fairness and Development — exploring how power is entrusted, used and abused within public and private institutions.", uae:"The UAE ratified the UN Convention against Corruption (UNCAC) in 2006, and UAE federal criminal law treats bribery as a serious offence; links to DP Global Politics (power, legitimacy) and Business Management (business ethics)."},
  words:[["Corruption","The abuse of entrusted power for private gain (the widely used Transparency International definition)."],["Bribery","Offering, giving, requesting or accepting something of value to improperly influence a decision."],["Embezzlement","Stealing or misusing money or property that a person has been trusted to manage."],["Nepotism","Favouring relatives or close friends, for example in jobs or contracts, regardless of merit."],["Conflict of interest","A situation where a person's private interests could improperly influence their professional duties."],["Extortion","Obtaining money or favours by using threats or the abuse of authority."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion B — Investigating", steps:["Choose ONE form of corruption (bribery, embezzlement, nepotism, conflict of interest or extortion) and ONE sector where it can occur (e.g. public procurement, healthcare, customs, sport, construction, education).","Research it using at least two reliable sources (e.g. UNODC, Transparency International, the OECD, reputable news organisations).","Record: how this form of corruption typically works in that sector, and who is harmed by it.","Identify ONE safeguard that institutions use to reduce this risk, and evaluate how effective it is likely to be."], prompts:["The form of corruption and sector I chose, and my two sources","How this form of corruption typically works in that sector","Who is harmed, directly and indirectly","One safeguard, and how effective it is likely to be"], success:"Good choices include bribery in public procurement (large contracts, many decision points), conflicts of interest in hiring, embezzlement of project funds, or match-fixing in sport. Strong answers explain the mechanism step by step (who holds the power, what is exchanged, how it is hidden) and identify indirect victims such as taxpayers, honest competitors or patients. For the top band, use two credible sources, avoid sensational claims, and evaluate the safeguard critically, for example noting that asset declarations only work if someone actually checks them."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"A procurement manager in a fictional public authority must choose a supplier for a new school-building project. She awards the contract to a firm owned by her brother-in-law. She does not declare the relationship, and the firm's bid was neither the cheapest nor the highest quality. When questioned, she says: “I took no money, so I have done nothing wrong.”", steps:["In a structured analytical paragraph (claim, evidence, analysis, conclusion), evaluate whether the manager's behaviour counts as corruption.","Use at least FOUR words from the Word Bank (e.g. corruption, nepotism, conflict of interest, entrusted power) accurately.","Respond directly to her defence that she “took no money”, and conclude with what she should have done."], model:"The manager's behaviour is a clear case of corruption, even though no money changed hands, because she abused power entrusted to her by a public authority to benefit a member of her family. Her decision combines nepotism, since a relative was favoured regardless of merit, with an undeclared conflict of interest, since her private loyalty influenced a professional duty. Her defence misunderstands the definition: private gain does not have to be cash, and benefits to family members or social standing still count. The harm is real, because public money was not spent on the best-value bid for a school. She should have declared the relationship immediately and withdrawn from the decision, allowing an independent panel to choose the supplier on merit.", rubric:[["1–2","Limited structure; little/no key vocabulary; judgement not explained."],["3–4","Basic structure; 1–2 key terms used; defence not addressed."],["5–6","Clear analytical structure; 3 key terms used accurately; defence addressed with some reasoning."],["7–8","Sophisticated, well-structured argument; 4+ key terms used precisely; defence convincingly rebutted; recommendation is specific and justified."]], starters:["The manager's behaviour is / is not corruption because ___.","This is an example of ___, since ___.","Her defence that she ‘took no money’ is flawed because ___.","She should have ___, which would have ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"Corruption Risk Map", steps:["In groups of 3–4, you will receive a profile of a fictional city with a ministry, a hospital, a port, a sports federation and a large construction company.","For each institution, identify the decision points where entrusted power could be abused (e.g. issuing licences, awarding contracts, hiring staff).","Rank the institutions from highest to lowest corruption risk, justifying your ranking with clear criteria (e.g. size of budgets, level of discretion, amount of oversight).","Propose ONE realistic safeguard for the highest-risk decision point.","Present your risk map in a 4-minute briefing, as if advising the city's audit authority. Use Word Bank vocabulary throughout."], rubric:[["1–2","Risk points vague or missing; ranking not justified."],["3–4","Some risk points identified; ranking has limited justification."],["5–6","Clear risk points for each institution; ranking justified with sensible criteria; relevant safeguard proposed."],["7–8","Insightful, precise risk analysis; criteria applied consistently; safeguard is realistic, specific and critically evaluated."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Name That Corruption", prompt:"Sort each case under the form of corruption it best illustrates.", bins:["Bribery","Embezzlement","Nepotism / conflict of interest"], items:[["A driver hands a traffic officer cash to avoid a fine",0],["A finance officer quietly transfers project funds into a personal account",1],["A manager hires her nephew over better-qualified applicants",2],["A company pays an inspector to ignore safety failures",0],["A charity treasurer uses donations to pay for a family holiday",1],["A council member votes to approve a project his own company will build, without declaring it",2],["An official uses a government car and fuel card for a private business",1],["A supplier offers a buyer ‘consultancy fees’ in exchange for winning a tender",0]]},
  quiz:[["Which definition of corruption is most widely used?",["Any illegal act committed by a government","The abuse of entrusted power for private gain","Any disagreement about how money is spent"],1],["What makes a gift likely to be a bribe?",["It is wrapped","It is given by a friend","It is intended to improperly influence a decision by someone with power"],2],["What is embezzlement?",["Stealing or misusing money or property a person has been trusted to manage","Favouring a relative for a job","Threatening someone to obtain money"],0],["A judge hearing a case involving her brother's company has…",["No issue, as long as she is fair","A conflict of interest she should declare, stepping away from the case","A duty to rule in her brother's favour"],1],["Why is public procurement considered a high-risk area for corruption?",["Large sums of money and many discretionary decision points are involved","It is never checked by anyone","Only junior staff make the decisions"],0],["In the extract, why is the manager's defence (“I took no money”) weak?",["Because she should have asked for money","Because procurement rules do not apply to family firms","Because private gain can include benefits to family, not just cash"],2],["Can corruption occur in the private sector?",["No, only governments can be corrupt","Yes, for example through bribery between companies or abuse of a role by employees","Only in very small companies"],1],["Which international agreement against corruption did the UAE ratify in 2006?",["The Paris Agreement","The Geneva Conventions","The UN Convention against Corruption (UNCAC)"],2]],
  exit:"In one sentence, explain why corruption does not always involve money changing hands.",
  copy:{facts:["Corruption is the abuse of entrusted power for private gain; ‘gain’ can include benefits for family, friends or status, not only cash.","Main forms include bribery, embezzlement, nepotism, conflicts of interest and extortion.","Corruption occurs in both public and private sectors, especially where large budgets meet high discretion and weak oversight; the UAE ratified UNCAC in 2006."]},
  next:"Next lesson: we continue Unit 1 by analysing why corruption happens — the pressures, opportunities and excuses behind it."
 },
 {
  id:"l2", lesson:2, title:"Why Does Corruption Happen?", unit:"Unit 1: Ethics in Our Contemporary Life", art:"reason",
  dna:"Recap: in one sentence, define corruption and name one of its forms. Now think: most people who commit corruption do not see themselves as ‘bad people’. Write down ONE excuse someone might tell themselves to justify accepting a bribe.",
  dnaQs:["Define corruption and name one of its forms (from last lesson).","Write ONE excuse someone might tell themselves to justify accepting a bribe.","Why might ordinary people convince themselves that corruption is acceptable?"],
  dnaHint:"Corruption is the abuse of entrusted power for private gain (e.g. bribery, embezzlement, nepotism). Common excuses include “everyone does it”, “I am underpaid, so I deserve it”, “it is only a small amount”, “nobody is really harmed” or “I will pay it back later”. These are examples of rationalisation — reasoning that makes wrong behaviour feel acceptable.",
  intentions:["Understand the individual, institutional and cultural causes of corruption.","Apply theoretical models, such as the fraud triangle and Klitgaard's formula, to explain why corruption occurs.","Evaluate which causes are most important and what this means for prevention."],
  criteria:["I can explain individual, institutional and cultural causes of corruption.","I can apply the fraud triangle and Klitgaard's formula to a case.","I can evaluate the relative importance of different causes and link them to prevention strategies."],
  concept:{key:"Causality", related:"Choice, Systems", soi:"Corruption results from the interaction of personal pressures, institutional opportunities and the excuses people use to justify wrongdoing.", context:"Fairness and Development — exploring how individual choices and institutional systems interact to produce unethical outcomes.", uae:"Islamic ethics stresses amanah (trustworthiness) and accountability before God; UAE institutions reduce opportunity through audit and digital government services; links to DP Psychology (moral disengagement) and Economics (incentives)."},
  words:[["Fraud triangle","A model (Cressey) stating that fraud usually requires pressure, opportunity and rationalisation together."],["Pressure","A personal motive, such as debt, greed or fear of failure, that pushes someone towards wrongdoing."],["Opportunity","Circumstances, such as weak oversight, that allow wrongdoing to happen without being detected."],["Rationalisation","Reasoning that makes wrong behaviour feel acceptable to the person doing it."],["Discretion","The freedom to make decisions according to one's own judgement."],["Normalisation","The process by which wrongdoing becomes seen as ordinary or expected within a group."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion B — Investigating", steps:["Research ONE of these explanations of corruption: the fraud triangle (Cressey), Klitgaard's formula (Corruption = Monopoly + Discretion − Accountability), or the idea of normalisation in organisations.","Use at least two reliable sources (e.g. academic summaries, UNODC, professional accounting bodies).","Record: what the model claims, and the key factors it identifies.","Apply the model to ONE real or realistic case of corruption, and note one limitation of the model."], prompts:["The model I chose and my two sources","What the model claims and its key factors","How the model explains my chosen case","One limitation of the model"], success:"Strong answers explain the model accurately: the fraud triangle focuses on the individual (pressure, opportunity, rationalisation), whereas Klitgaard's formula focuses on the institution (monopoly power and discretion increase risk; accountability reduces it). For the top band, apply the model precisely to a case and identify a genuine limitation — for example, the fraud triangle may underestimate organisational culture, or Klitgaard's formula is a simplification that cannot be measured numerically."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"A licensing clerk in a fictional municipality is the only person who approves business permits. Nobody reviews his decisions, and applications can take months. He is struggling with family debts. Applicants begin offering “speed payments” to get their permits faster, and he tells himself: “I'm not refusing anyone — I'm just helping people who are in a hurry.”", steps:["In a structured analytical paragraph, use the fraud triangle to explain why the clerk becomes corrupt, identifying the pressure, opportunity and rationalisation.","Use Klitgaard's formula to explain what the institution got wrong.","Use at least FOUR words from the Word Bank accurately, and conclude by identifying which factor would be the most effective to change."], model:"The clerk's corruption can be explained using the fraud triangle: his family debts create pressure, his position as the only approver with no review gives him opportunity, and his belief that he is ‘just helping people in a hurry’ is a clear rationalisation that disguises bribery as service. Klitgaard's formula shows that the institution also failed: the clerk held a monopoly over permits, had complete discretion over timing, and faced no accountability because nobody reviewed his decisions. While his personal pressure is real, the most effective factor to change is the opportunity, because the institution can control it directly — for example by introducing an online system with fixed timelines and random audits — whereas it cannot remove every employee's financial difficulties.", rubric:[["1–2","Limited structure; models not used or used incorrectly."],["3–4","Basic structure; one model applied partially; 1–2 key terms used."],["5–6","Clear structure; both models applied with some accuracy; 3 key terms used; a factor is chosen."],["7–8","Sophisticated analysis; both models applied precisely; 4+ key terms used accurately; choice of factor is convincingly justified."]], starters:["Using the fraud triangle, the pressure is ___, the opportunity is ___ and the rationalisation is ___.","Klitgaard's formula shows that the institution ___.","Although ___, the most effective factor to change is ___ because ___.","For example, the municipality could ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"Redesign the System", steps:["In groups of 3–4, choose ONE fictional high-risk process (e.g. issuing driving licences, hospital appointment waiting lists, university admissions, customs clearance).","Diagnose the process using Klitgaard's formula: where is the monopoly, where is the discretion, and where is accountability missing?","Redesign the process to reduce corruption risk (e.g. digital services, rotation of staff, published criteria, independent review).","Identify ONE unintended consequence your redesign might create (e.g. slower service, excluding people without internet access) and how you would manage it.","Present your before-and-after process in a 4-minute pitch, using Word Bank vocabulary."], rubric:[["1–2","Diagnosis missing or unclear; redesign is vague."],["3–4","Partial diagnosis; redesign addresses some risks; unintended consequence not considered."],["5–6","Clear diagnosis using the model; realistic redesign; unintended consequence identified and addressed."],["7–8","Precise, insightful diagnosis; redesign is realistic, specific and well-justified; unintended consequences evaluated thoughtfully."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Fraud Triangle Sort", prompt:"Sort each factor into the part of the fraud triangle it represents.", bins:["Pressure","Opportunity","Rationalisation"], items:[["Large personal debts",0],["Nobody checks the accounts",1],["“Everyone in this department does it”",2],["Fear of losing status if targets are missed",0],["One person controls both payments and records",1],["“The company underpays me, so I deserve this”",2],["No whistleblowing channel exists",1],["“It's only a small amount — nobody is really harmed”",2]]},
  quiz:[["What are the three elements of the fraud triangle?",["Pressure, opportunity and rationalisation","Money, power and fame","Greed, luck and timing"],0],["According to Klitgaard's formula, corruption increases with…",["More accountability","More monopoly and discretion, and less accountability","More competition and transparency"],1],["“Everyone does it, so it's not really wrong” is an example of…",["Opportunity","Pressure","Rationalisation"],2],["What does 'normalisation' of corruption mean?",["Making corruption illegal","Wrongdoing becoming seen as ordinary or expected within a group","Reporting corruption to the authorities"],1],["In the extract, what gave the clerk the opportunity to become corrupt?",["His family debts","He was the only approver and nobody reviewed his decisions","Applicants were in a hurry"],1],["Why do institutions often focus on reducing opportunity?",["It is the factor they can control most directly","Pressure and rationalisation do not matter","It is the cheapest option in every case"],0],["Which is an example of an institutional cause of corruption?",["A person's greed","A person's personal debts","Complex procedures with no oversight"],2],["The Islamic value of amanah relates to corruption because it emphasises…",["Speed in completing tasks","Trustworthiness in handling what has been entrusted to you","Competition between employees"],1]],
  exit:"Which is the most important cause of corruption — the person or the system? Answer in one justified sentence.",
  copy:{facts:["The fraud triangle states that corruption usually needs pressure, opportunity and rationalisation together.","Klitgaard's formula: Corruption = Monopoly + Discretion − Accountability, which shows why institutional design matters.","Causes are individual (greed, need), institutional (weak oversight, too much discretion) and cultural (normalisation, ‘everyone does it’)."]},
  next:"Next lesson: we continue Unit 1 by evaluating the consequences of corruption for individuals, societies and nations."
 },
 {
  id:"l3", lesson:3, title:"The Consequences of Corruption", unit:"Unit 1: Ethics in Our Contemporary Life", art:"globe",
  dna:"Recap: in one sentence, name the three parts of the fraud triangle. Now think: a bribe of a few hundred dirhams is paid so that a building passes a safety inspection. Write down TWO people who might be harmed — one obvious, one less obvious.",
  dnaQs:["Name the three parts of the fraud triangle (from last lesson).","Name one OBVIOUS person harmed by the inspection bribe.","Name one LESS OBVIOUS person or group harmed, and explain how."],
  dnaHint:"The fraud triangle is pressure, opportunity and rationalisation. Obvious victims include the people who will live or work in the unsafe building. Less obvious victims include honest builders who lose business because they paid for proper safety work, insurers, emergency services, and the wider public, whose trust in all inspections is damaged.",
  intentions:["Understand the economic, social, political and personal consequences of corruption.","Analyse how small acts of corruption can produce large, widespread harm.","Evaluate the claim that corruption is a ‘victimless crime’."],
  criteria:["I can explain economic, social, political and personal consequences of corruption.","I can trace how one corrupt act leads to a chain of wider harms.","I can construct a reasoned evaluation of the claim that corruption is victimless."],
  concept:{key:"Global interactions", related:"Consequences, Equity", soi:"Corruption damages economies, weakens trust in institutions and deepens inequality, so its harms extend far beyond the people directly involved.", context:"Fairness and Development — exploring how unethical decisions affect development, equity and social trust.", uae:"Transparency International's Corruption Perceptions Index (CPI) shows how perceived corruption affects a country's reputation and investment; the UAE consistently performs strongly in the Gulf region; links to DP Economics (development, investment) and Global Politics (legitimacy)."},
  words:[["Public trust","The confidence citizens have that institutions will act fairly and honestly."],["Inequality","Unfair differences in wealth, opportunity or access to services between groups."],["Rule of law","The principle that everyone, including leaders, is equally subject to fair and publicly known laws."],["Legitimacy","The general acceptance that an authority has the right to govern or make decisions."],["Opportunity cost","What is lost when resources are used in one way instead of the best alternative."],["Ripple effect","The way one action spreads outwards to cause many further consequences."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion B — Investigating", steps:["Choose ONE area where the consequences of corruption are well documented: healthcare, infrastructure and construction, education, the environment, sport, or foreign investment.","Research it using at least two reliable sources (e.g. Transparency International, the World Bank, UNODC, reputable news organisations).","Record: how corruption in this area harms people, and whether the harm is economic, social, political or personal.","Explain how the Corruption Perceptions Index (CPI) measures corruption, and identify ONE limitation of measuring corruption by perception."], prompts:["The area I chose and my two sources","How corruption harms people in this area","Type(s) of harm: economic, social, political, personal","How the CPI works, and one limitation of measuring perception"], success:"Strong answers give specific mechanisms, such as diverted health budgets leading to medicine shortages, or bribed inspections leading to unsafe buildings. The CPI ranks countries by perceived public-sector corruption using expert and business surveys. For the top band, use two credible sources and evaluate a genuine limitation — for example, perception may differ from reality, it focuses on the public sector, and it may not capture corruption that a country's companies commit abroad."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"A columnist writes: “Corruption is a victimless crime. When a businessman pays an official to speed up a permit, both sides are happy — the businessman saves time and the official earns a little extra. Nobody is hurt.”", steps:["Write a structured evaluative paragraph (or two) responding to the columnist's claim.","Acknowledge any part of the claim that seems reasonable, then challenge it using at least TWO types of consequence (economic, social, political or personal).","Use at least FOUR words from the Word Bank accurately, and reach a clear, justified conclusion."], model:"The columnist is right that, in the moment, both people in the exchange may feel they have benefited, which is exactly why this kind of corruption is so persistent. However, the claim that ‘nobody is hurt’ ignores the ripple effect. Economically, every applicant who cannot or will not pay is pushed to the back of the queue, so honest businesses face delays and costs that corrupt competitors avoid, and investors may avoid a system they see as unfair. Socially and politically, each payment teaches citizens that rules can be bought, which erodes public trust and weakens the rule of law, because the law no longer applies equally to everyone. Over time, this undermines the legitimacy of the institution itself. Corruption is therefore not victimless; its victims are simply less visible than the people who profit from it.", rubric:[["1–2","Limited structure; claim accepted or rejected without reasoning."],["3–4","Basic structure; one type of consequence discussed; 1–2 key terms used."],["5–6","Clear evaluative structure; two types of consequence discussed; 3 key terms used; clear conclusion."],["7–8","Sophisticated, balanced evaluation; concession and rebuttal well handled; 4+ key terms used precisely; conclusion is compelling and justified."]], starters:["The columnist is right that ___; however, ___.","Economically, this exchange harms ___ because ___.","Socially and politically, each payment ___.","Corruption is therefore not victimless, because ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"The Ripple Effect Investigation", steps:["In groups of 3–4, you will receive ONE fictional corrupt act (e.g. a bribed exam official, a falsified environmental report, a rigged hospital equipment tender).","Create a ripple-effect diagram tracing the consequences outwards: immediate, short-term, long-term, and national or international.","Label each consequence as economic, social, political or personal.","Identify the point in the chain where an intervention (an audit, a whistleblower, a journalist) could have stopped the harm.","Present your diagram in a 4-minute briefing, using Word Bank vocabulary."], rubric:[["1–2","Few consequences identified; little connection between them."],["3–4","Some consequences identified; chain partly logical; limited labelling."],["5–6","Clear, logical chain across several levels; consequences correctly labelled; a sensible intervention point identified."],["7–8","Insightful, well-reasoned chain including less obvious consequences; precise labelling; intervention point convincingly justified."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Type of Consequence", prompt:"Sort each consequence of corruption by its main type.", bins:["Economic","Social","Political"], items:[["Foreign companies decide not to invest in a country",0],["Citizens stop believing that hard work leads to success",1],["People lose confidence that elections or courts are fair",2],["Public money is wasted on overpriced projects",0],["The gap between rich and poor grows wider",1],["An authority loses its legitimacy in the eyes of citizens",2],["Honest businesses lose contracts to those who pay bribes",0],["Poorer families cannot access services without paying extra",1]]},
  quiz:[["What is meant by the ‘ripple effect’ of corruption?",["One corrupt act spreading outwards to cause many further harms","Corruption only affecting the people involved","Corruption disappearing over time"],0],["Which is an economic consequence of corruption?",["Loss of trust in courts","Reduced foreign investment and wasted public funds","Greater respect for the law"],1],["Why does corruption often increase inequality?",["Because everyone pays the same bribes","Because it only happens in wealthy countries","Because those who can pay gain access that others are denied"],2],["What does the Corruption Perceptions Index (CPI) measure?",["The exact number of bribes paid in a country","Perceived levels of public-sector corruption, based on expert and business assessments","How many people are in prison for corruption"],1],["What is one limitation of measuring corruption by perception?",["Perception may differ from the actual level of corruption","Perception is always exactly accurate","It measures only private companies"],0],["How does corruption weaken the rule of law?",["By making laws longer","By making courts faster","By showing that rules can be bought, so the law no longer applies equally"],2],["In the extract, what does the columnist overlook?",["That the businessman saved time","The less visible victims, such as honest applicants and public trust","That the official earned extra money"],1],["Why might a less obvious victim of a bribed safety inspection be an honest builder?",["They lose business to competitors who cut corners and pay bribes instead","They receive the bribe","They never work on buildings"],0]],
  exit:"Complete the sentence: “Corruption is not a victimless crime because ___.”",
  copy:{facts:["Corruption has economic (waste, lost investment), social (inequality, lost trust), political (weaker rule of law and legitimacy) and personal (prosecution, lost reputation) consequences.","Even small acts of corruption create ripple effects that harm people who were never part of the exchange.","Transparency International's CPI measures perceived public-sector corruption, which affects a country's reputation and attractiveness for investment."]},
  next:"Next lesson: we continue Unit 1 by examining unethical behaviour in education — plagiarism, cheating and the misuse of AI."
 },
 {
  id:"l4", lesson:4, title:"Unethical Behaviour in Education", unit:"Unit 1: Ethics in Our Contemporary Life", art:"pen",
  dna:"Recap: in one sentence, give one economic and one social consequence of corruption. Now think: a student pastes an essay question into an AI tool, lightly edits the answer and submits it as their own work. Write down: is this the same as copying a classmate? Why or why not?",
  dnaQs:["Give one economic and one social consequence of corruption (from last lesson).","Is submitting edited AI-generated work the same as copying a classmate? Why or why not?","Who is harmed when a student submits work that is not their own?"],
  dnaHint:"Economic: wasted public money or lost investment; social: inequality or lost trust. Submitting unacknowledged AI work is a form of academic misconduct because the student presents ideas and wording they did not produce as their own — just as with copying a classmate. Those harmed include the student (who does not learn), honest classmates (whose fair results are devalued) and the trust that gives qualifications their value.",
  intentions:["Understand the main forms of academic misconduct, including plagiarism, collusion, contract cheating and AI misuse.","Analyse the link between academic dishonesty and corruption in wider society.","Evaluate where the line lies between ethical and unethical uses of AI in learning."],
  criteria:["I can define and distinguish between forms of academic misconduct.","I can explain how academic dishonesty connects to corruption and its consequences.","I can make and justify judgements about ethical and unethical uses of AI in academic work."],
  concept:{key:"Identity", related:"Integrity, Responsibility", soi:"Academic integrity protects the value of learning and qualifications, and the habits students form in education shape the integrity they carry into adult life.", context:"Scientific and Technical Innovation — exploring how new technologies such as generative AI challenge traditional ideas of honest, original work.", uae:"UAE qualifications are formally verified and attested for employment and further study, so their value depends on honest assessment; links to the IB's academic integrity expectations, Theory of Knowledge (knowledge and technology) and the Extended Essay."},
  words:[["Academic integrity","Acting honestly, fairly and responsibly in all learning and assessment."],["Plagiarism","Presenting someone else's words, ideas or work as one's own without acknowledgement."],["Collusion","Working with others to produce work that is then submitted as one's own individual work."],["Contract cheating","Paying or asking another person or service to complete assessed work for you."],["Fabrication","Inventing or falsifying data, results or sources."],["Acknowledgement","Clearly crediting the sources, people or tools (including AI) used in producing work."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion B — Investigating", steps:["Research the IB's expectations on academic integrity, and how they apply to the use of AI tools in coursework (e.g. Internal Assessments, the Extended Essay, TOK essays).","Use at least two reliable sources (e.g. the IB's published guidance, a university academic integrity policy, reputable education news).","Record: which uses of AI are generally considered acceptable, and which are considered misconduct.","Research ONE form of corruption in education beyond the classroom (e.g. selling exam papers, fake certificates, bribery for admissions) and note its consequences."], prompts:["My two sources","Uses of AI generally considered acceptable","Uses of AI considered academic misconduct","One form of corruption in education, and its consequences"], success:"Strong answers recognise that AI is not banned outright: using it to brainstorm, check understanding or improve study skills can be acceptable, while presenting AI-generated text, ideas or analysis as one's own work without acknowledgement is misconduct. AI-generated material that is used must be clearly credited, and the work must still be the student's own. For the top band, use two credible sources and explain clearly why fake certificates or bought exam papers harm employers, patients, clients and honest graduates — not just the cheater."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"A DP student is behind on her Internal Assessment. She uses an AI tool to generate an analysis section, then paraphrases it carefully so that it “sounds like her”. She does not mention the AI in her work. She argues: “The ideas are just general knowledge, and I rewrote every sentence myself.”", steps:["Write a structured evaluative paragraph explaining whether the student has committed academic misconduct.","Respond directly to both parts of her defence (“general knowledge” and “I rewrote every sentence”).","Use at least FOUR words from the Word Bank accurately, and conclude with what she should have done instead."], model:"The student has committed academic misconduct, because she has presented analysis that she did not produce as her own work without any acknowledgement. Her first defence fails because an analysis section is not general knowledge: it is the part of an Internal Assessment where a student's own thinking is assessed. Her second defence also fails, since paraphrasing does not remove the need for acknowledgement — rewording someone else's ideas without credit is still plagiarism, whether the source is a person or an AI tool. This matters beyond one grade: it undermines academic integrity, treats honest classmates unfairly, and devalues a qualification that universities and employers trust. She should have spoken to her teacher about the deadline and, if she used AI at all, acknowledged it clearly and produced the analysis herself.", rubric:[["1–2","Limited structure; judgement not explained; little key vocabulary."],["3–4","Basic structure; one part of the defence addressed; 1–2 key terms used."],["5–6","Clear evaluative structure; both parts of the defence addressed; 3 key terms used; sensible recommendation."],["7–8","Sophisticated, precise evaluation; both defences convincingly rebutted; 4+ key terms used accurately; wider consequences considered."]], starters:["The student has / has not committed academic misconduct because ___.","Her claim that the ideas are ‘general knowledge’ is flawed because ___.","Paraphrasing does not remove the need for acknowledgement because ___.","Instead, she should have ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"An AI Charter for Our Year Group", steps:["In groups of 3–4, draft a clear AI-use charter for Year 13 students, covering coursework, homework and revision.","Divide uses of AI into three categories: clearly acceptable, acceptable only with acknowledgement, and not acceptable — with at least three examples in each.","Justify each category using principles from today's lesson (honesty, fairness, learning, acknowledgement).","Identify ONE grey area your group disagreed about, and explain how you resolved it.","Present your charter in a 4-minute pitch; the class will vote on the clearest and fairest charter. Use Word Bank vocabulary throughout."], rubric:[["1–2","Categories unclear; examples vague; little justification."],["3–4","Basic categories with some examples; limited justification."],["5–6","Clear categories with realistic examples; justified using lesson principles; grey area identified."],["7–8","Precise, practical charter; consistent principled justification; grey area discussed with genuine nuance."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Integrity or Misconduct?", prompt:"Sort each action: does it show academic integrity, or is it academic misconduct?", bins:["Academic integrity","Academic misconduct"], items:[["Using AI to generate practice questions for revision",0],["Submitting an AI-written paragraph as your own analysis",1],["Citing all sources, including an AI tool you used, in your bibliography",0],["Paying an online service to write your Extended Essay",1],["Discussing ideas with a classmate, then writing your own work independently",0],["Inventing data for a lab report because the experiment failed",1],["Sharing your completed IA with a friend so they can ‘check their structure’",1],["Asking your teacher for an extension when you are genuinely struggling",0]]},
  quiz:[["What is plagiarism?",["Presenting someone else's words, ideas or work as your own without acknowledgement","Studying with a friend","Asking your teacher for help"],0],["Paying someone else to complete your coursework is called…",["Collusion","Contract cheating","Acknowledgement"],1],["Is paraphrasing AI-generated text without acknowledgement acceptable?",["Yes, as long as every sentence is reworded","Yes, if the AI tool is free","No — rewording someone else's ideas without credit is still plagiarism"],2],["What is collusion?",["Working with others to produce work then submitted as your own individual work","Inventing data","Citing your sources"],0],["Which use of AI is most likely to be acceptable?",["Generating your IA analysis","Generating revision questions to test your own understanding","Writing your TOK essay for you"],1],["How does academic dishonesty connect to wider corruption?",["It is completely unrelated","It only matters inside one school","It devalues qualifications and builds habits of dishonesty people may carry into work"],2],["Why are fake certificates harmful to society?",["They put unqualified people in roles where others depend on their skills","They save people time","They only harm the person who bought them"],0],["What should a student do if they are behind on a deadline?",["Use AI to produce the work secretly","Copy a friend's draft","Speak to their teacher honestly and ask for support"],2]],
  exit:"Write ONE use of AI you think is acceptable in your studies and ONE you think is not, with a reason for each.",
  copy:{facts:["Academic misconduct includes plagiarism, collusion, contract cheating, fabrication and presenting AI-generated work as your own.","Any AI-generated material used in work must be clearly acknowledged, and the thinking assessed must be the student's own.","Academic dishonesty devalues qualifications that universities and employers rely on, and builds habits that can lead to corruption later in life."]},
  next:"Next lesson: we continue Unit 1 by exploring unethical behaviour in the workplace and how professionals respond to it."
 },
 {
  id:"l5", lesson:5, title:"Unethical Behaviour in the Workplace", unit:"Unit 1: Ethics in Our Contemporary Life", art:"business",
  dna:"Recap: in one sentence, define plagiarism and contract cheating. Now think: it is your first week in a new job. A supplier sends you an expensive gift voucher “to welcome you to the team”. Write down what you would do, and what you would want to know before deciding.",
  dnaQs:["Define plagiarism and contract cheating (from last lesson).","What would you do with the supplier's gift voucher?","What would you want to know before deciding?"],
  dnaHint:"Plagiarism is presenting someone else's work as your own without acknowledgement; contract cheating is paying someone to do your work. For the voucher, a strong answer considers: the company's gifts policy, the value of the gift, whether you make decisions about this supplier, and whether accepting it could create an obligation. The safest professional response is usually to declare it to your manager and follow the policy.",
  intentions:["Understand common forms of unethical behaviour in the workplace, such as bribery, kickbacks, fraud, conflicts of interest and misuse of resources.","Analyse the role of codes of conduct, gifts policies and whistleblowing in maintaining workplace ethics.","Evaluate how professionals should respond to ethical grey areas and pressure from colleagues or managers."],
  criteria:["I can identify and explain forms of unethical workplace behaviour.","I can explain how codes of conduct and whistleblowing protect organisations and the public.","I can evaluate the best response to a workplace ethical dilemma and justify my decision."],
  concept:{key:"Responsibility", related:"Integrity, Accountability", soi:"Workplace ethics depends both on individual integrity and on organisational systems that make it safe to do the right thing.", context:"Globalisation and Sustainability — exploring how businesses and organisations act responsibly in a connected global economy.", uae:"UAE federal criminal law treats both offering and accepting a bribe as offences, as well as acting as an intermediary; UAE organisations commonly use codes of conduct and declarations of interest; links to DP Business Management (ethics, CSR) and the work ethic valued in Islamic tradition (itqan — excellence and sincerity in work)."},
  words:[["Code of conduct","A written set of rules and values describing expected behaviour in an organisation."],["Kickback","A secret payment given to someone in return for arranging a contract or deal."],["Whistleblower","A person who reports wrongdoing within an organisation to those who can act on it."],["Gifts and hospitality policy","Rules about which gifts, meals or favours employees may accept, and when they must be declared."],["Misappropriation","Using an organisation's money, time or resources for unauthorised personal purposes."],["Due diligence","Careful checks carried out before a decision, such as investigating a supplier's honesty."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion B — Investigating", steps:["Find ONE real, publicly available code of conduct or ethics policy from a large company or public organisation.","Use the code itself plus at least one other reliable source (e.g. a news article or business ethics guide).","Record: what the code says about gifts, conflicts of interest, bribery and reporting concerns.","Evaluate ONE strength and ONE weakness of the code: would it actually guide an employee facing a difficult decision?"], prompts:["The organisation and code I chose, and my second source","What it says about gifts and conflicts of interest","What it says about bribery and reporting concerns","One strength and one weakness of the code"], success:"Strong answers quote or closely paraphrase specific rules (e.g. a value limit for gifts, a requirement to declare interests, a confidential reporting line) rather than summarising vaguely. For the top band, evaluate critically: a code is only effective if employees are trained in it, if leaders model it, and if people who raise concerns are protected from retaliation. A code that is long, vague or never enforced may exist ‘on paper only’."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"A junior accountant in a fictional company notices that her manager has been approving payments to a supplier for services that were never delivered. When she raises it, the manager says: “This is how the business works here. Keep quiet and you'll be promoted next year. Make trouble and you won't last long.”", steps:["Write a structured evaluative response explaining what forms of unethical behaviour are taking place.","Evaluate at least TWO possible responses the accountant could take (e.g. stay silent, confront the manager again, report through an internal channel, report externally).","Use at least FOUR words from the Word Bank accurately, and conclude with the response you think is most justified, considering both ethics and her own protection."], model:"The manager appears to be involved in fraud, and possibly a kickback arrangement, because payments are being made for services that were never delivered, which is a misappropriation of company funds. His comment is also an attempt to pressure the accountant through a mixture of reward and threat, and his claim that ‘this is how the business works’ is a classic rationalisation. Staying silent might protect her career in the short term, but it would make her complicit and leave her exposed if the fraud is later discovered. Confronting the manager again is unlikely to work, since he has already shown he will not act. The most justified response is to keep careful, factual records and report the issue through the company's confidential whistleblowing channel or to its audit or compliance function, as set out in its code of conduct. If internal channels fail, she may need to seek external advice. This response meets her ethical duty while giving her the greatest protection.", rubric:[["1–2","Limited structure; unethical behaviour not clearly identified; one response only."],["3–4","Basic structure; behaviour partly identified; two responses mentioned briefly; 1–2 key terms."],["5–6","Clear structure; behaviour accurately identified; two responses evaluated; 3 key terms used; justified conclusion."],["7–8","Sophisticated evaluation; behaviour precisely identified; several responses weighed thoughtfully; 4+ key terms used accurately; conclusion balances ethics and self-protection convincingly."]], starters:["The manager's behaviour involves ___ because ___.","His comment that ‘this is how the business works’ is an example of ___.","If she stayed silent, ___; however, ___.","The most justified response is ___ because ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"Ethics Training Workshop", steps:["In groups of 3–4, imagine you are the ethics and compliance team of a fictional company that is about to expand into a new market.","Write THREE short, realistic workplace scenarios (e.g. a gift from a supplier, a request to ‘speed up’ a customs payment, a manager hiring a relative).","For each scenario, write the ‘right response’ according to a strong code of conduct, and explain why.","Design one simple tool for employees (e.g. a flowchart: ‘Should I accept this gift?’ or a five-question ethics test).","Deliver your workshop to the class in 5 minutes, involving the audience in at least one scenario. Use Word Bank vocabulary throughout."], rubric:[["1–2","Scenarios unrealistic or unclear; responses not justified."],["3–4","Basic scenarios; responses partly justified; tool is unclear."],["5–6","Realistic scenarios; responses justified with reference to ethics and policy; useful, clear tool."],["7–8","Highly realistic, nuanced scenarios including grey areas; responses convincingly justified; tool is practical, clear and genuinely useful."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Acceptable, Unethical or Grey Area?", prompt:"Sort each workplace situation: is it acceptable, clearly unethical, or a grey area you should declare or ask about first?", bins:["Acceptable","Clearly unethical","Grey area — declare or ask first"], items:[["Accepting a branded pen at a conference",0],["Taking a secret payment for recommending a supplier",1],["Being offered an expensive dinner by a supplier during a tender",2],["Reporting a concern through the company's confidential hotline",0],["Claiming expenses for a trip you did not take",1],["Your cousin applies for a job in a team you manage",2],["Using company time and equipment to run your own side business",1],["Completing a supplier due diligence check before signing a contract",0]]},
  quiz:[["What is a kickback?",["A secret payment given in return for arranging a contract or deal","A company bonus for good work","A type of contract"],0],["What is the main purpose of a code of conduct?",["To make employees work longer hours","To describe expected behaviour and help employees make ethical decisions","To replace the law"],1],["Who is a whistleblower?",["A manager who sets targets","A supplier who offers discounts","A person who reports wrongdoing to those who can act on it"],2],["Using company money for unauthorised personal purposes is called…",["Due diligence","Misappropriation","Acknowledgement"],1],["What is the best first step if a supplier offers you a valuable gift?",["Accept it quietly","Check the gifts policy and declare it to your manager","Give it to a colleague"],1],["In the extract, why is staying silent a risky choice for the accountant?",["It makes her complicit and exposed if the fraud is later discovered","It guarantees her promotion","It is required by law"],0],["Why must organisations protect whistleblowers from retaliation?",["Because whistleblowers are always right","So that people feel safe to report wrongdoing early","Because reporting is never confidential"],1],["Under UAE federal criminal law, who can commit a bribery offence?",["Only the person who accepts the bribe","Only the person who offers the bribe","Both the person who offers and the person who accepts, as well as intermediaries"],2]],
  exit:"Write ONE question you would ask yourself before accepting any gift at work.",
  copy:{facts:["Unethical workplace behaviour includes bribery, kickbacks, fraud, undeclared conflicts of interest and misappropriation of resources.","Codes of conduct, gifts policies, due diligence and protected whistleblowing channels help employees do the right thing.","UAE federal criminal law treats both offering and accepting a bribe as offences; the Islamic value of itqan calls for sincerity and excellence in work."]},
  next:"Next lesson: we complete Unit 1 by exploring how to promote ethical behaviour and fight corruption — in ourselves and in others."
 },
 {
  id:"l6", lesson:6, title:"Promoting Ethical Behaviour and Fighting Corruption", unit:"Unit 1: Ethics in Our Contemporary Life", art:"toolkit",
  dna:"Unit 1 vocabulary check! Define each term as precisely as you can: Corruption, Rationalisation, Rule of law, Academic integrity, Whistleblower. This is our final lesson of Unit 1 — let's see how much has stuck.",
  dnaQs:["Corruption and Rationalisation — what does each mean?","Rule of law and Academic integrity — what does each mean?","Whistleblower — what does it mean?"],
  dnaHint:"Corruption: the abuse of entrusted power for private gain. Rationalisation: reasoning that makes wrong behaviour feel acceptable. Rule of law: everyone, including leaders, is equally subject to fair and publicly known laws. Academic integrity: acting honestly, fairly and responsibly in learning and assessment. Whistleblower: a person who reports wrongdoing to those who can act on it.",
  intentions:["Understand the personal, institutional, national and international strategies used to fight corruption.","Analyse the role of UAE bodies and international agreements in promoting accountability and transparency.","Evaluate which strategies are most effective, and commit to concrete personal actions that promote integrity."],
  criteria:["I can explain anti-corruption strategies at personal, institutional, national and international levels.","I can describe the role of UAE accountability bodies and UNCAC in fighting corruption.","I can evaluate the effectiveness of different strategies and set a realistic personal integrity commitment."],
  concept:{key:"Change", related:"Accountability, Integrity", soi:"Fighting corruption requires both strong institutions that hold power to account and individuals with the moral courage to act with integrity.", context:"Fairness and Development — exploring how societies build transparent, accountable institutions and ethical citizens.", uae:"The UAE Accountability Authority (formerly the State Audit Institution) and the Abu Dhabi Accountability Authority audit public funds and promote transparency; the UAE ratified UNCAC in 2006; International Anti-Corruption Day is marked every year on 9 December."},
  words:[["Transparency","Openness about decisions, rules and the use of resources, so that others can see and check them."],["Accountability","The obligation to explain and take responsibility for one's decisions and actions."],["Oversight","Monitoring and supervision of an organisation or official by an independent body."],["Audit","An official, independent examination of an organisation's accounts and activities."],["Moral courage","The willingness to act rightly despite risk, pressure or disapproval."],["Integrity","Consistency between one's moral principles and one's actions, even when nobody is watching."]],
  task1:{title:"Research Task", mins:20, criterion:"Criterion A — Knowing and Understanding", steps:["Choose ONE anti-corruption body or agreement: the UAE Accountability Authority, the Abu Dhabi Accountability Authority, the UN Convention against Corruption (UNCAC), or Transparency International.","Research it using at least two reliable sources (e.g. its official website, UNODC, reputable news organisations).","Record: its purpose, what powers or tools it uses, and who it holds to account.","Evaluate ONE factor that makes it effective, and ONE factor that limits what it can achieve."], prompts:["The body or agreement I chose, and my two sources","Its purpose","The powers or tools it uses, and who it holds to account","One factor that makes it effective, and one that limits it"], success:"Strong answers distinguish clearly between types of actor: accountability and audit authorities examine how public money is spent; UNCAC is an international treaty committing states to prevention, criminalisation, international cooperation and the recovery of stolen assets; Transparency International is a non-governmental organisation that researches and campaigns. For the top band, use official sources accurately, avoid unsupported claims, and evaluate thoughtfully — for example, an audit body's impact depends on independence and on whether its findings lead to action."},
  task2:{title:"Literacy Task", mins:25, criterion:"Criterion C — Communicating", extract:"A Year 13 student says: “Corruption is a problem for governments and big companies to solve. There's nothing an ordinary person like me can do about it.”", steps:["Write a structured argumentative paragraph (or two) responding to this claim, drawing on learning from across Unit 1.","Acknowledge the role of institutions, then argue for the role of the individual, using at least TWO concrete examples.","Use at least FIVE words from across Unit 1's Word Bank (any lesson) accurately, and conclude with ONE realistic personal commitment."], model:"It is true that institutions such as the UAE Accountability Authority, courts and international agreements like UNCAC are essential, because only they have the power to audit public funds, investigate wrongdoing and prosecute offenders. However, the claim that individuals can do nothing overlooks how corruption actually works. As the fraud triangle shows, corruption depends on rationalisation, and every person who refuses to accept that ‘everyone does it’ weakens the normalisation that allows it to spread. Individuals also act as the first line of defence: a student who maintains academic integrity, an employee who declares a conflict of interest, or a whistleblower who reports fraud through the proper channels can stop harm before it ripples outwards. Fighting corruption therefore requires both strong oversight and personal moral courage. My commitment is to acknowledge every source and tool I use in my coursework this year, even when nobody would notice if I did not.", rubric:[["1–2","Limited structure; claim accepted or rejected without reasoning; little vocabulary."],["3–4","Basic structure; some reasoning; one example; 2–3 key terms used."],["5–6","Clear argumentative structure; institutions acknowledged; two concrete examples; 4 key terms used; realistic commitment."],["7–8","Sophisticated, well-structured argument drawing on several lessons; 5+ key terms used precisely; examples insightful; commitment specific and genuine."]], starters:["It is true that institutions such as ___ are essential because ___.","However, the claim that individuals can do nothing overlooks ___.","For example, a student / employee who ___ can ___.","My personal commitment is to ___."]},
  task3:{title:"Project Task (Unit Showcase)", mins:"2 lessons", criterion:"Criterion D — Thinking Critically", name:"International Anti-Corruption Day Campaign", steps:["In groups of 3–4, design a school campaign for International Anti-Corruption Day (9 December), aimed at younger students in the secondary school.","Choose ONE focus from Unit 1 (e.g. academic integrity and AI, the hidden victims of corruption, saying no to ‘small’ bribes, speaking up safely).","Create a campaign package: a key message, a poster or short video script, and ONE interactive activity suitable for a younger year group.","Explain how you will measure whether your campaign has changed attitudes (e.g. a before-and-after survey).","Pitch your campaign to the class in 5 minutes; the strongest campaign may be shared with the school's student leadership team. Use Word Bank vocabulary from across Unit 1."], rubric:[["1–2","Campaign message unclear; weak link to unit learning; no evaluation plan."],["3–4","Basic campaign with some link to unit learning; activity or evaluation underdeveloped."],["5–6","Clear, age-appropriate campaign strongly linked to unit learning; engaging activity; sensible evaluation plan."],["7–8","Compelling, creative and realistic campaign drawing on several lessons; highly engaging activity; thoughtful, measurable evaluation plan."]]},
  game:{type:"sort", title:"Which Level of Action?", prompt:"Sort each anti-corruption action by the level at which it works.", bins:["Personal integrity","Institutional / national safeguard","International cooperation"], items:[["Refusing to share your coursework with a friend to copy",0],["An accountability authority auditing how a ministry spends public funds",1],["Countries cooperating to recover stolen assets hidden abroad",2],["Declaring a conflict of interest before a decision",0],["A company setting up a confidential whistleblowing hotline",1],["States committing to the UN Convention against Corruption",2],["Moving government services online to reduce face-to-face ‘speed payments’",1],["Saying no when a colleague says ‘everyone does it’",0]]},
  quiz:[["What does 'transparency' mean in fighting corruption?",["Openness about decisions and the use of resources, so others can check them","Keeping all decisions private","Making decisions very quickly"],0],["What is the main role of the UAE Accountability Authority?",["Writing school textbooks","Auditing public funds and promoting accountability and transparency","Running private companies"],1],["When is International Anti-Corruption Day marked each year?",["1 January","2 December","9 December"],2],["What is UNCAC?",["A UAE company","An international treaty committing states to prevent and fight corruption","A type of audit"],1],["Why does moving government services online reduce corruption risk?",["It reduces face-to-face discretion and creates clear records","It makes officials earn more","It removes all rules"],0],["Which is an example of moral courage?",["Following the crowd to avoid trouble","Ignoring wrongdoing you notice","Reporting wrongdoing through proper channels despite pressure to stay silent"],2],["Why is the individual important in fighting corruption, according to this unit?",["Individuals cannot make any difference","Refusing to rationalise or normalise wrongdoing stops corruption spreading","Only governments can recognise corruption"],1],["Which statement best summarises Unit 1?",["Corruption is only a legal issue for governments","Ethics is only about following school rules","Fighting corruption needs strong institutions and individuals with integrity"],2]],
  exit:"Write ONE specific, realistic commitment you will make this year to act with integrity, and explain why it matters.",
  copy:{facts:["Corruption is fought at four levels: personal integrity, institutional safeguards, national bodies and international cooperation.","The UAE Accountability Authority and the Abu Dhabi Accountability Authority audit public funds; the UAE ratified UNCAC in 2006; International Anti-Corruption Day is 9 December.","Transparency, accountability and independent oversight reduce opportunity, while individual moral courage challenges rationalisation and normalisation."]},
  next:"Unit 1: Ethics in Our Contemporary Life is complete! Next, we begin the next unit of the Grade 12 Moral Education course."
 }
];
